---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import androidJoin from "../../../video/public/android-join.png";
import androidWebsites from "../../../video/public/android-websites.png";
import iphoneIcloud from "../../../video/public/iphone-icloud.png";
import iphoneImport from "../../../video/public/iphone-import.png";
import iphoneDuration from "../../../video/public/iphone-duration-45.png";
import iphoneSchedules from "../../../video/public/iphone-schedules.png";
import macAddDevice from "../../../video/public/mac-add-device.png";
import macExport from "../../../video/public/mac-export.png";
import macIcloud from "../../../video/public/mac-icloud.png";
import macDuration from "../../../video/public/mac-duration-45.png";
import macSchedules from "../../../video/public/mac-schedules.png";
import macWebsites from "../../../video/public/mac-websites.png";

const releases = "https://github.com/dees91/posato/releases/latest";
const appStore = "https://apps.apple.com/app/posato/id6812237585";
---

<Base description="Posato blocks the websites and apps you choose for a timed pause on your Mac, iPhone, Linux computer, or Android phone, now or on a schedule. No Posato account, no analytics, and no Posato-operated server.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <h1>A little space.<br /><span>For what matters.</span></h1>
      <div class="hero-details">
      <p class="lead">
        Posato blocks the websites and apps you choose for a timed pause on your Mac, iPhone, Linux
        computer, or Android phone, now or on a schedule, within the <a href="#limits">limits</a> below.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">No Posato account, no analytics, and no Posato-operated server.</p>
      <p class="download">
        <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-mac.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Mac" /></a>
        <a href={appStore}><img src="/badges/download-on-the-app-store.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato on the App Store" /></a>
        <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-linux.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Linux" /></a>
        <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-android.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Android" /></a>
      </p>
      <p class="platforms">macOS 13 or later on Apple silicon or Intel, iOS 18 or later, Ubuntu 24.04 or later, and Android 13 or later. <a href="#install">How to install</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-demo">
      <video
        class="hero-video"
        autoplay
        controls
        muted
        loop
        playsinline
        preload="metadata"
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        poster="/media/hero-poster.jpg"
        aria-label="Posato demo: choose websites, pick 45 minutes, and start a pause on a Mac; one pause set change reaching a Mac, an iPhone, a Linux computer, and an Android phone; optional sync through iCloud or a folder you choose, encrypted on your device"
      >
        <source src="/media/hero.mp4" type="video/mp4" media="(min-width: 701px)" />
      </video>
      <img
        class="hero-poster"
        src="/media/hero-poster.jpg"
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        alt="Posato on a Mac reporting restrictions active, with the session end time and an option to end early"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
</section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="choose">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="choose">Choose what to pause</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Group what you want to pause into pause sets, such as one for work and one for evenings. A set
      holds exact website domains and, on each device, the apps you choose there. One set is the
      default; pick another when you start a pause or plan a schedule. App choices stay on the device
      where you made them.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macWebsites}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="Mac showing the pause set Focus with the sample domains example.com and example.net"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={androidWebsites}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="Android phone showing the pause set Focus with the sample domains example.com and example.net"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="start">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="start">Start a timed pause</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Choose a pause set and a duration: a quick choice from 25 minutes to 8 hours,
      <strong>Until end of day</strong>, or any length from <strong>5 minutes to 24 hours</strong>
      (up to 23 hours 59 minutes on iPhone). Review your
      choices and start the session. Posato blocks your chosen websites and apps on that device, within the <a href="#limits">limits</a> below. The session ends at the selected time or when you deliberately end
      it early. On iPhone, restrictions can linger after the session ends. On a Mac, a one-time setup
      lets pauses and schedules start without asking for your password each time.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macDuration}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="Session setup on a Mac with the pause set Focus and a 45-minute pause selected"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={iphoneDuration}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="Session setup on an iPhone with a 45-minute pause selected"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="schedules">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="schedules">Plan pauses ahead</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Add a schedule with a name, a pause set, weekdays, and hours, such as weekday mornings from 9 to
      11. It starts and ends on its own on each device you set up, even with Posato's window closed on a
      computer or the app closed on a phone. Skip the next one or end one early when plans change. When pauses overlap,
      Posato pauses everything in their sets, within each device's limits, and each item stays paused
      until the last pause that includes it ends. On Mac and iPhone, Posato can tell you when a pause ends or when one starts on another device.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macSchedules}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="Mac Schedules screen with a weekday schedule named Deep work and a daily one named Evening reading"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={iphoneSchedules}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="iPhone Schedules screen with a weekday schedule named Deep work"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">Screenshots are from the real apps and show sample choices.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="sync">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="sync">Share the session with iCloud</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Choose <strong>Sync with iCloud</strong> on one Mac and one iPhone signed in to the same Apple
      Account to share your pause sets with their websites, sessions, and schedules. No QR code or
      invitation is needed. Sync is best effort, so a change may arrive late or not at all. A session
      started on your iPhone blocks on the Mac only after you resume it there. Update Posato on every
      device: once one device runs Posato 1.3, devices on an earlier version stop syncing until they
      update.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macIcloud}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="The iCloud row on a Mac, linked: the latest sync attempt completed, the time it last synced, Sync now, and Remove workspace"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={iphoneIcloud}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="The same iCloud workspace on an iPhone, with the time it last synced, Sync now, and Remove workspace"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="folder">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="folder">Sync through a folder</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      To link any mix of Mac, iPhone, Linux, and Android, keep the workspace in a folder that Dropbox,
      OneDrive, Syncthing, iCloud Drive, or another service keeps in sync. Posato writes only encrypted
      files there. A workspace uses iCloud or one folder, never both. Create the workspace on one
      device, then choose <strong>Add a device</strong> there. On the other device, take two steps in
      either order: choose its copy of the same folder, and scan the QR code with the phone's camera,
      open the shared link, or type the pairing code. It joins as soon as it has both. The code works
      for 10 minutes. A device that joins during a pause starts pausing right away.
      You cannot remove a single device; to stop sharing with one, remove the workspace and create a
      new one in another folder.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macAddDevice}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="Add a device on a Mac, with a QR code and a one-time pairing code"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={androidJoin}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="An Android phone joining the folder workspace: the folder is chosen and the pairing code step is open"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="export">
    <div class="step-copy">
    <h2 id="export">Copy pause sets with a file</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      To set up a device outside your workspace, such as a Mac on another Apple Account, choose
      <strong>Export pause sets…</strong> from the <strong>…</strong> menu in <strong>Pause sets</strong>.
      The <code>.posato</code> file holds your pause sets with their websites, the default set, and your
      schedules, never app choices. <strong>Protect with a code</strong> encrypts it with a
      nine-character code that Posato shows once. On the other device, choose <strong>Import…</strong>
      to add the sets to yours or replace them; imported schedules arrive switched off. It is a one-time
      copy, not sync.
    </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <div class="device device-desktop shot-desktop">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={macExport}
            widths={[480, 636, 960, 1272]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 70vw, 36rem"
            alt="Export pause sets on a Mac with Protect with a code switched on and a sample nine-character code shown"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="device device-phone shot-phone">
        <div class="device-screen">
          <Image
            src={iphoneImport}
            widths={[160, 330, 660]}
            sizes="(max-width: 600px) 24vw, 12rem"
            alt="Importing on an iPhone: Add to my pause sets is selected over Replace my pause sets, and the schedule arrives switched off"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <p class="prose">Posato adds deliberate friction; it is not a lock you cannot open.</p>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>
        <strong>Mac:</strong> blocking works while Posato runs, including in the menu bar with its
        window closed. Quitting Posato stops it and new scheduled starts. Blocking needs administrator
        approval; after the one-time setup, starts no longer ask for it. Paused apps are quit, so save
        your work before a session starts. Website blocking
        covers Safari and Google Chrome Stable while they use the system proxy settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>iPhone:</strong> Restrictions can linger after a session ends. For sessions under 15
        minutes, they clear only when Posato is open at the end or the next time you open it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Linux:</strong> websites are blocked by name through <code>/etc/hosts</code>, with no
        pause page; a browser with its own DNS over HTTPS bypasses it. Paused apps are ended, so save
        your work before a session starts. A system service, installed once with your password, ends
        the pause on time even when Posato is closed. Closing the window keeps Posato running with an
        icon in the status area. On a desktop whose status area does not show app icons, such as GNOME
        without the AppIndicator extension, there is no icon, and Posato stays in the background only
        while a pause runs or a schedule is on. Quitting Posato stops new scheduled starts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Android:</strong> websites are blocked by name through a local VPN, with no pause page;
        another VPN cannot run during a pause, and strict Private DNS or a browser with its own DNS over
        HTTPS bypasses it. A paused app is covered by a block screen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sync:</strong> Sync is best effort; a change may arrive late or not at all. If every copy of your workspace key is lost,
        synchronized data cannot be recovered.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="prose">
      Read the <a href="/limits/">full blocking and sync limits</a> for browser coverage, VPN and Private Relay behavior, resuming after sleep, folder sync and pairing codes, and data recovery.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="privacy-section" aria-labelledby="privacy">
    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
    <p class="prose">
      Posato's developer does not receive your data. Your pause sets, websites, app choices, sessions,
      and schedules stay on your devices. If you turn on iCloud sync, changes are encrypted on your device before they are
      stored in your private iCloud database. With folder sync, Posato writes only encrypted files to the folder you choose. On Mac, update checks go to GitHub Releases only after you agree.
      See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="note" aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install">Install Posato</h2>
    <p class="download download-row">
      <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-mac.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Mac" /></a>
      <a href={appStore}><img src="/badges/download-on-the-app-store.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato on the App Store" /></a>
      <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-linux.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Linux" /></a>
      <a href={releases}><img src="/badges/download-for-android.svg" width="186" height="56" alt="Download Posato for Android" /></a>
    </p>
    <ul class="install">
      <li>
        <h3>Mac</h3>
        <p>
          macOS 13 or later. Download <code>Posato-1.5.0.dmg</code> for Apple silicon or
          <code>Posato-1.5.0-intel.dmg</code> for an Intel Mac from <a href={releases}>GitHub Releases</a>,
          signed and notarized, and move Posato to Applications. It can check for new releases after you
          agree, and installing an update waits until your session has ended. To move from Posato 1.0,
          quit Posato and replace the application.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>iPhone</h3>
        <p>iOS 18 or later. Install Posato from the <a href={appStore}>App Store</a>; it updates there.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Linux</h3>
        <p>
          Ubuntu 24.04 or later. Download <code>posato_1.5.0_arm64.deb</code> for arm64 or
          <code>posato_1.5.0_amd64.deb</code> for x86-64 from <a href={releases}>GitHub Releases</a> and
          install it with <code>sudo apt install ./posato_1.5.0_&lt;arch&gt;.deb</code>. Then open Posato
          and follow the first run; it asks for your password once to install the pausing service.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Android</h3>
        <p>
          Android 13 or later. On the phone, download <code>Posato-1.5.0.apk</code> from
          <a href={releases}>GitHub Releases</a> and open it. When Android asks, allow installing apps from
          this source, then install, open Posato, and follow the first run, which asks for the permissions
          Posato needs to pause.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      To update on Linux or Android, install the newer package the same way. The
      <a href="https://github.com/dees91/posato/blob/main/docs/product/limits-and-platforms.md#supported-platforms">platform matrix</a>
      records what was checked on each platform, including that the Intel build was checked under Rosetta, not on an Intel Mac.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
